      "5": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "SELECTION\n\nSelection menus are a frequent sight in patches.\nYou might want the user to select a shape, sorting order or direction.\nFor this you can create buttons and dropdown menus.\n\nTo do this, you first create an input node that supports options.\nFor example: Int In, Float In, String In, Color In.\nSelect the node and in the node panel navigate to \"Option Count\".\nHere you'll fill in how many options/entries you need and choose what value each option holds and how it should be called.\nFinally you can select whether it should be represented by a dropdown or button interface.\n\nDo note that if the text gets too big for a button, Wire will default to a dropdown instead.\n\nIn the patch below we've created some examples.\nOn the left side you'll see a variety of input nodes in both button and dropdown configuration.\n\nOn the right side you'll see a more practical example.\nHere we've created an Int In node to select which blend mode we want from the Transition node.\nThe Transition node supports 22 blend modes, but we've imagined that we only need 5 of them.\nTo achieve this we've adjusted the value of each option.\nClick on the node to see how each option has a different value.\n\nAssignment: create a Color In node, set the option count to 2, the option view to button,  assign colors and text."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 1133.754638671875,
          "width": 819.1238403320312,
          "x": 528.3616333007812,
          "y": 672.0215454101562
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ffae7bff",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment",
        "thumbnail_visible": true
      },
      "51": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "OUTPUTS\n\nOutput nodes are a category of nodes that send data from Wire.\n\nThe most commonly used output node is the Texture Out node.\nThis is the final node of all patches that produce an image.\nIn other words: what goes into the Texture Out node is what Arena/Avenue will display.\n\nIn case of an effect you would use the Texture In to get the image of the currently played clip in, do some processing in Wire and send it out again using the Texture Out node.\n\nWire is also able to send out OSC or MIDI data by using the OSC Out and MIDI Out nodes."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 686.56640625,
          "width": 805.38623046875,
          "x": 528.1510620117188,
          "y": -69.0348892211914
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
