      "7": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "SLIDERS\n\nParameters are often controlled by sliders.\nOpacity, Intensity, Frequency, these are the kind of things you might want to have on a slider.\n\nThere are two nodes that can produce sliders for you: Float In and Int In.\nUse integers when you need whole numbers and float when you need a decimal point.\n\nTo create a slider, click on the node and navigate to the node panel on the right.\nMake sure that \"Has Min\" and \"Has Max\" are both enabled.\nAlso make sure that Min is not equal or larger than Max.\n\nWe've created a couple of sliders below.\nThe first two are simple sliders that run from 0 to their max.\nThe second two are unipolar, they produce both positive and negative values.\n\nThe third pair has a unit assigned to them.\nYou can do this in the node panel under \"Unit\" , units are purely cosmetic in Wire.\n\nThe final node is renamed to \"Float Flipped\" and is connected to the 1-x node.\nThe 1-x node takes 1 and subtracts the input value.\nThis is an easy way to \"flip\" a slider.\nThe user sees 0 to 1, but we as designers can work with the reverse.\n\nAssignment: create an Int In node that runs from 1 to 100, rename it to Time and set the unit to milliseconds."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 1134.13720703125,
          "width": 757.5567016601562,
          "x": -260,
          "y": 672
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "fff26eb5",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment",
        "thumbnail_visible": true
      },
      "71": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "RESOURCES\n\nResource nodes allow users to import their own resources into the patch.\nWe create a resource input node in Wire and on the Arena/Avenue side the user can just drag and drop resources into your effects, sources or mixers.\n\nResources are not loaded into memory.\nThey are a reference to a file on your hard drive.\nThis makes them incredibly lightweight.\nHaving 100 file paths in memory is a LOT more efficient than having 100 video files in memory.\n\nWe'll get back to using resources and how to use them in more advanced tutorials.\nBut now you know that they exist and how they work on a fundamental level."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 899.203125,
          "width": 820.060791015625,
          "x": 1390.9013671875,
          "y": 908.1514892578125
